TI  - Study on the Process of Neutralizing and Degumming of Grape Seed Oil
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE SEVENTH INTERNATIONAL SYMPOSIUM ON VITICULTURE AND ENOLOGY (2011)
CP  - 7th International Symposium on Viticulture and Enology
AB  - A lot of by-products including grape seeds, grape pomace and wine lee, were produced during wine-making, but they are usually thrown away or used as fertilizers after fermentation. The improper disposal of winery wastes will cause waste of resources and pollute environment. Many researchers have found that there have rich fatty acid in grape seeds, including the first most abundant Linoleic acid accounted for about 78% of unsaturated fatty acid. Furthermore, there have plenty of vitamin containing vitamin A, E, D, K, P and mineral containing Ca, Fe, Zn, Mg, Cu, K, Na, Mn, Co, et al. These substances have multifunctions, such as antioxidation, preventing cardiovascular disease, scavenging free radical and hairdressing. the process of neutralizing and degumming of grape seed oil were systematic examined in this study. The results were found: The rate of extracting grape seed oil with general oil crusher was about 5.5%; The optimum process of neutralizing of grape seed oil was using 18% of NaOH and 0.3% of extra dose of NaOH at 45 degrees C of the beginning of neutralizing the oil; and the optimum process of degumming was added 4-flod of water for lecithoid concentration of the oil at 80 degrees C for 9h. Finally, the acid value and lecithoid content of the refining grape oil reached the secondary national standard of cooking oil.
