AU  - Barnett, MPG
AU  - Roy, NC
AU  - McNabb, WC
TI  - Influence of nutritional factors on genetic and epigenetic events relevant to chronic diseases
T2  - AGRO FOOD INDUSTRY HI-TECH
AB  - An increasing literature base points to the role of inadequate diet in the development of non-communicable diseases in humans. However, minor variations in genotype, in the form of variant single nucleotide polymorphisms, may lead to substantial variations in individual requirements for different nutrients. While an increased ratio of omega-3 to omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids is likely to benefit most of the population, those who carry a variant allele in the Apolipoprotein E gene may actually be disadvantaged by such an increase. Folate provides an example where current population levels may already be slightly sub-optimal for many populations in the developed world. Those individuals who carry certain variant alleles of methylene tetrahydrofolate reductase may need substantially more folate than the rest of the population, and without supplementation, this could substantially increase their risk of chronic diseases. The cellular levels of nutrients such as folate not only directly affect metabolism, but may also impact the expression of certain genes through epigenetic effects. These reversible changes in gene function, or other cellular phenotype, occur without a change in DNA sequence ( genotype). There is a developing recognition that prenatal nutrition significantly impacts the response to postnatal feeding, and that epigenetic events are important modulators of the lifetime risk of chronic disease. The technologies and models are becoming available to understand these interactions. It will become possible to devise genome-based nutritional interventions to prevent, delay, and treat diseases such as asthma, obesity, Type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and prostate cancer. This will have substantial economic impacts in both developing and developed nations.
