TI  - THROMBOSIS IN DOG PRODUCED BY SINGLE RAPID INFUSIONS OF LONG CHAIN SATURATED FATTY ACIDS
T2  - AMERICAN JOURNAL OF THE MEDICAL SCIENCES
AB  - To determine whether long chain saturated fatty acids, which are known to enhance solidification in vitro, can produce thrombosis in vivo, 195 dogs were infused intravenously over a 5-min. period with 10 ml/kg of a 0.1% suspension of the Na salts of oleic, linolenic, stearic or palmitic acid and also a combination of oleic-palmitic and oleic stearic. Saline was used as a control. Oleic acid, linolenic acid and normal saline were innocuous. Twenty-five per cent of stearic-acid infused dogs, 33% of palmitic, 10% of oleic-palmitic and 71% of oleic-stearic infused dogs died. Massive thrombosis (venous and arterial) was found at necropsy in over 80% of these dogs. In those that survived the infusion, immediate necropsy after the Wessler technique frequently showed small localized thromboses. The only laboratory test significantly different from the normal was the recalcification time which was shortened in dogs given the saturated fatty acids. The significance and possible mechanism of these results are discussed.
