TI  - References values with liability - guideline values of fat intake
T2  - ERNAHRUNGS UMSCHAU
AB  - The new D-A-CH-Reference Values of the German Nutrition Society and the Nutrition Societies of Austria and Switzerland follow earlier recommendations to decrease the risk of coronary heart disease (CHD) by reduction of fat intake to a moderate level of 30 % of energy. Saturated fatty acids should be limited to 10 % and polyunsaturated fatty acids should provide 7 % of energy. The ratio of saturated to unsaturated fatty acids should be 1 : 2 at least and the ratio of n-6 to n-3 fatty acids 5 : 1. Trans unsaturated fatty acids should not exceed I % of energy and dietary cholesterol should be limited to 300 mg per day The wholesome diet recommended is also rich in dietary fiber. In the present article, results of prospective cohort studies and secondary prevention studies with clinical or angiographic endpoints are presented to support the reference values. These studies have provided strong evidence that an increasing intake of polyunsaturated, especially n-3 unsaturated fatty acids from fish or plants at an optimal ratio to n-6 fatty acids, substantially lowers the risk of CHD. A decrease of total fat intake by reduction of saturated fat - and a hight fiber content - are also important for the prevention of overweight.
