TI  - Mechanistic Characterization Regarding the Effect of Supra-Optimal Nitrogen: A Case Study for <i>Suaeda salsa</i>
T2  - JOURNAL OF PLANT GROWTH REGULATION
AB  - There are serious problems with sudden mortality of natural populations of Suaeda salsa (S. salsa) in Liaohe estuary wetland, Liaodong Bay, China, due to the presence of high nitrogen as runoffs from upstream ago-ecosystems. This study attempts to comprehensively understand the mechanism regarding the effect of this supra-optimal nitrogen on S. salsa based on growth and metabolic perturbations based on RNA-seq studies. The effect of nitrogen types was further delimited by the application of nitrate and ammonium nitrogen treatments. We found that nitrate nitrogen decreased the mass, sucrose, starch, soluble protein, and indoleacetic acid (IAA) content of S. salsa. Nitrate nitrogen down regulated the genes encoding a-linolenic acid metabolism at 1 day. Glycolate oxidase activity increased under 1 mM nitrate nitrogen. Further ammonium nitrogen also caused a decrease in the height, mass, sucrose, starch, soluble protein, and IAA content of S. salsa. The genes encoding photosystem II, photosystem I, photosynthetic electron transport, and cytochrome b6f complex were down regulated under 0.5 mM ammonium nitrogen. Ammonium nitrogen down regulated the genes encoding a-linolenic acid metabolism at 1 day. Ammonium nitrogen decreased the activity of adenosine triphosphate synthase and Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase at 40 d. Therefore, our studies provide insights into the degradation of high nitrogen-infested S. salsa ecosystem that appeared primarily due to a disrupted photosynthesis and plant hormone signal transduction.
