TI  - YELLOW FAT IN THE MINK
T2  - JOURNAL OF NUTRITION
AB  - A disease in young mink known as " yellow fat" occurs in the late summer months when the kits are growing rapidly and is characterized by a paralysis before death, a marked subcut. edema and a yellow coloration of the depot fat. This disease was produced experimentally by feeding 3% linolenic acid as raw linseed oil in addition to a horse meat-cereal-liver diet. The addition of alpha-tocopherol to the exptl. diet prevented the occurrence of the disease. Spectrophotometric analysis of the depot fat showed that high amts. of trienoic acid are deposited by the mink when fed either horse meat with a high fat content or horse meat with a normal fat content plus raw linseed oil, in addition to the cereal-liver diet. When horse meat with a normal fat content is fed, lower amts. of trienoic acid are deposited, and very low amts. of trienoic acid are deposited when cottonseed oil is fed to mink.
